1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,300
Delhi, India.

2
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:08,443
In the courtyard of a ruined mosque, a dark iron column rises about six meters above the ground.

3
00:00:08,443 --> 00:00:12,833
It has been out in the open for some sixteen hundred years.

4
00:00:12,833 --> 00:00:16,033
Monsoons, heat, and the smog of a megacity.

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:19,155
And the internet says it never rusted.

6
00:00:19,155 --> 00:00:19,993
Not a flake.

7
00:00:19,993 --> 00:00:23,347
Some say ancient smiths knew a lost super-metal.

8
00:00:23,347 --> 00:00:27,400
Others say the metal did not come from this planet at all.

9
00:00:27,866 --> 00:00:32,780
Tonight we handle it like an insurance adjuster handles a warranty.

10
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:36,226
Sixteen hundred years, no rust, no maintenance.

11
00:00:36,226 --> 00:00:41,066
A claim that big needs paperwork, and every clause gets inspected.

12
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:42,197
Approved.

13
00:00:42,197 --> 00:00:42,714
Denied.

14
00:00:42,714 --> 00:00:44,782
Or approved with conditions.

15
00:00:44,782 --> 00:00:52,166
No opinions and no mood, only what the laboratory, the inscription, and the iron itself can back up.

16
00:00:52,633 --> 00:01:01,843
And by the end, you will know exactly why this pillar survived, and why the real answer is a better story than the one with the aliens.

17
00:01:01,843 --> 00:01:02,866
File the claim.

18
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:07,958
Item one.

19
00:01:07,958 --> 00:01:08,445
Intake.

20
00:01:08,445 --> 00:01:14,490
From its top to the bottom of its base, the pillar measures seven point two one meters.

21
00:01:14,490 --> 00:01:19,423
It is about forty centimeters wide, and it weighs more than six tonnes.

22
00:01:19,423 --> 00:01:22,133
More than a meter of it is underground.

23
00:01:22,599 --> 00:01:27,288
It stands in the Qutb complex, in Mehrauli, in the south of Delhi.

24
00:01:27,288 --> 00:01:33,113
Right beside it, a minaret and the ruins of a mosque built in the eleven hundreds.

25
00:01:33,113 --> 00:01:35,599
The pillar is older than all of it.

26
00:01:36,066 --> 00:01:41,320
At the top, a carved bell-shaped capital, about thirty centimeters tall.

27
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:48,033
The shaft below it is smooth, dark, and almost black, with a faint shine like old gun metal.

28
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:51,076
Millions of people have come to see it.

29
00:01:51,076 --> 00:01:54,644
And the claim they carry with them is always the same.

30
00:01:54,644 --> 00:01:56,428
This iron has never rusted.

31
00:01:56,428 --> 00:01:59,600
So that is clause one, and it is where we begin.

32
00:02:00,066 --> 00:02:00,776
Clause one.

33
00:02:00,776 --> 00:02:02,455
Does it really never rust?

34
00:02:02,455 --> 00:02:04,133
Let's inspect the surface.

35
00:02:04,599 --> 00:02:05,377
Clause one.

36
00:02:05,377 --> 00:02:07,002
The pillar never rusts.

37
00:02:07,002 --> 00:02:08,345
First, the surface.

38
00:02:08,345 --> 00:02:12,020
Look closely and you do not see bare, shining metal.

39
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:16,966
You see a dark skin of corrosion, thin and smooth, covering all of it.

40
00:02:17,433 --> 00:02:19,377
This is the first correction.

41
00:02:19,377 --> 00:02:21,655
The pillar does rust, very slowly.

42
00:02:21,655 --> 00:02:29,966
What it does not do is what ordinary iron does out in the weather, which is keep rusting, flake by flake, until it crumbles.

43
00:02:30,433 --> 00:02:36,894
Ordinary iron makes a loose, flaky rust that holds water and lets the damage move deeper.

44
00:02:36,894 --> 00:02:42,266
A little rain, a little oxygen, and a few decades later, the post is gone.

45
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:44,778
The pillar's skin is different.

46
00:02:44,778 --> 00:02:50,058
It is compact, tightly stuck to the metal, and it protects what lies underneath.

47
00:02:50,058 --> 00:02:51,575
How thick has it grown?

48
00:02:51,575 --> 00:02:52,565
Sources differ.

49
00:02:52,565 --> 00:02:57,448
Estimates run from about a twentieth of a millimeter to half a millimeter.

50
00:02:57,448 --> 00:02:58,966
Either way, it is thin.

51
00:02:59,433 --> 00:03:10,972
Much of what we know comes from Professor R Balasubramaniam of the Indian Institute of Technology, Kanpur, who spent years studying the rust of the pillar.

52
00:03:10,972 --> 00:03:12,833
We will keep meeting him.

53
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:14,944
There is also a tradition.

54
00:03:14,944 --> 00:03:21,523
For generations, visitors stood with their backs to the pillar and tried to clasp their hands behind it.

55
00:03:21,523 --> 00:03:24,433
If your fingers met, you would have good luck.

56
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:34,220
In nineteen ninety-seven, India's Archaeological Survey built an iron grill around the pillar to protect it from all those hands.

57
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:37,833
And that gave scientists an accidental experiment.

58
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:42,457
The rubbing had polished a bright patch about five feet up.

59
00:03:42,457 --> 00:03:52,745
According to a study Balasubramaniam published in two thousand, within about a year of the fence, that bright patch had turned a thin, dark brown.

60
00:03:52,745 --> 00:03:54,999
Fresh iron, rebuilding its skin.

61
00:03:55,466 --> 00:03:57,056
Verdict on clause one.

62
00:03:57,056 --> 00:04:00,235
Denied as written, approved with conditions.

63
00:04:00,235 --> 00:04:04,426
It rusts, slowly, and then the rust stops being a problem.

64
00:04:04,426 --> 00:04:06,233
The real question is how.

65
00:04:06,699 --> 00:04:07,423
Clause two.

66
00:04:07,423 --> 00:04:09,067
Nobody knows who made it.

67
00:04:09,067 --> 00:04:14,000
Most of the claims say it is a mystery, but the pillar is signed, in a way.

68
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,433
On the shaft there is an inscription.

69
00:04:16,899 --> 00:04:21,437
A short poem in Sanskrit, cut into the iron in Gupta-era script.

70
00:04:21,437 --> 00:04:25,265
The letters are small, and they are still clear today.

71
00:04:25,265 --> 00:04:26,966
The iron kept them safe.

72
00:04:27,433 --> 00:04:32,618
In J F Fleet's classic translation, the text praises a king named Chandra.

73
00:04:32,618 --> 00:04:41,307
It says he conquered enemies in Bengal, that his fame reached the southern ocean, and that he was devoted to the god Vishnu.

74
00:04:41,307 --> 00:04:43,199
It never names his dynasty.

75
00:04:43,666 --> 00:04:51,328
Scholars match him to Chandragupta the Second, who ruled around three seventy-five to four fifteen C E.

76
00:04:51,328 --> 00:04:55,568
His gold coins show an archer and the short name Chandra.

77
00:04:55,568 --> 00:04:58,766
The script and the poetry fit the same age.

78
00:04:59,233 --> 00:05:03,858
An East India Company officer copied the text in eighteen thirty-one.

79
00:05:03,858 --> 00:05:12,640
James Prinsep printed an incomplete version in eighteen thirty-four, and his full reading and translation in eighteen thirty-eight.

80
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:17,333
It took until eighteen seventy-five to read the king's name correctly.

81
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:22,981
The inscription says the pillar was raised on a hill called Vishnupada.

82
00:05:22,981 --> 00:05:32,833
Researchers think that was Udayagiri, a hill of rock-cut caves in central India, where Chandragupta's own inscriptions have been found.

83
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:35,412
So how did it end up in Delhi?

84
00:05:35,412 --> 00:05:37,173
Honestly, we do not know.

85
00:05:37,173 --> 00:05:42,173
One view says the Tomar king Anangpal moved it in the eleventh century.

86
00:05:42,173 --> 00:05:52,033
Another, argued by historian Finbarr Barry Flood, says the sultan Iltutmish carried it off after sacking Vidisha, in the thirteenth century.

87
00:05:52,500 --> 00:06:00,191
If it really came from Udayagiri, someone moved more than six tonnes of iron across hundreds of kilometers.

88
00:06:00,191 --> 00:06:05,366
And in the courtyard of a mosque, historians read it as a trophy of war.

89
00:06:05,833 --> 00:06:07,425
Verdict on clause two.

90
00:06:07,425 --> 00:06:08,076
Approved.

91
00:06:08,076 --> 00:06:12,057
A real king, a real date range, and a real inscription.

92
00:06:12,057 --> 00:06:15,024
The pillar is not an orphan from nowhere.

93
00:06:15,024 --> 00:06:16,399
It has a signature.

94
00:06:16,866 --> 00:06:17,694
Clause three.

95
00:06:17,694 --> 00:06:22,086
Nobody could have built something this big sixteen hundred years ago.

96
00:06:22,086 --> 00:06:23,168
We can test that.

97
00:06:23,168 --> 00:06:26,733
Because the pillar carries the marks of how it was made.

98
00:06:27,199 --> 00:06:30,682
Ancient Indian iron was never melted and poured.

99
00:06:30,682 --> 00:06:40,766
Ore was reduced with charcoal in small furnaces, and what came out was a spongy lump of iron, called a bloom, that still held bits of slag.

100
00:06:41,233 --> 00:06:48,467
To build something bigger, you joined lumps while they were white hot, and hammered them until they fused into one.

101
00:06:48,467 --> 00:06:49,851
That is forge welding.

102
00:06:49,851 --> 00:06:52,933
And this pillar is made of that, lump after lump.

103
00:06:53,399 --> 00:07:03,286
According to Balasubramaniam, the pillar was probably built lying on its side, with lumps of hot iron added from the side and hammered in.

104
00:07:03,286 --> 00:07:06,366
The joints still leave traces on the shaft.

105
00:07:06,833 --> 00:07:14,441
In his observations, those forge-welded joints rust darker than the rest, which is how researchers can see them.

106
00:07:14,441 --> 00:07:17,566
The finished surface was then hammered smooth.

107
00:07:18,033 --> 00:07:19,339
It was a huge job.

108
00:07:19,339 --> 00:07:23,838
It took a team, a lot of charcoal, and an organised workforce.

109
00:07:23,838 --> 00:07:24,999
Remarkable, yes.

110
00:07:24,999 --> 00:07:31,166
But it is exactly the kind of organised effort that empires of that age were good at.

111
00:07:31,633 --> 00:07:33,225
And Delhi is not alone.

112
00:07:33,225 --> 00:07:41,394
At Dhar, in central India, lie the broken pieces of another iron pillar, traditionally linked to the eleventh century.

113
00:07:41,394 --> 00:07:45,133
Together the pieces measure more than thirteen meters.

114
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:51,013
That is about twice the height of Delhi's, and heavier, at around seven tonnes.

115
00:07:51,013 --> 00:07:55,056
It was probably the largest forged iron pillar of its time.

116
00:07:55,056 --> 00:07:56,633
It was never a one-off.

117
00:07:56,633 --> 00:07:57,866
The skill existed.

118
00:07:58,333 --> 00:08:00,033
Verdict on clause three.

119
00:08:00,033 --> 00:08:00,529
Denied.

120
00:08:00,529 --> 00:08:07,754
Ancient smiths could and did forge massive iron, and the pillar still wears the marks of their method.

121
00:08:07,754 --> 00:08:09,666
The how is not the mystery.

122
00:08:10,133 --> 00:08:11,027
Clause four.

123
00:08:11,027 --> 00:08:15,200
The metal itself has a secret chemistry, a lost formula.

124
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:18,256
Here, at last, the claim has a real core.

125
00:08:18,256 --> 00:08:23,100
Because the iron is unusual, and the lab can tell us exactly how.

126
00:08:23,566 --> 00:08:28,000
The pillar is almost pure wrought iron, with very little carbon.

127
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,978
But it has an unusual amount of phosphorus.

128
00:08:30,978 --> 00:08:33,126
About a quarter of one percent.

129
00:08:33,126 --> 00:08:36,866
Modern iron usually carries less than a fifth of that.

130
00:08:37,333 --> 00:08:37,609
Why?

131
00:08:37,609 --> 00:08:43,194
In a modern furnace, limestone is added to carry the phosphorus away in the slag.

132
00:08:43,194 --> 00:08:46,022
The ancient smiths did not use limestone.

133
00:08:46,022 --> 00:08:50,366
They used wood and ore, and the phosphorus stayed in the metal.

134
00:08:50,833 --> 00:08:53,493
Some researchers add another detail.

135
00:08:53,493 --> 00:09:00,957
The fuel may have included phosphorus-rich woods, such as Cassia auriculata, a common shrub in India.

136
00:09:00,957 --> 00:09:04,799
Charcoal from it would push even more phosphorus in.

137
00:09:05,266 --> 00:09:10,889
The iron also holds tiny specks of slag, glassy leftovers from the smelting.

138
00:09:10,889 --> 00:09:13,035
Normally, impurities are bad.

139
00:09:13,035 --> 00:09:18,066
Here, they turn out to matter, because they change how rust behaves.

140
00:09:18,533 --> 00:09:21,833
Balasubramaniam's team proposed the sequence.

141
00:09:21,833 --> 00:09:24,033
At first, ordinary rust forms.

142
00:09:24,033 --> 00:09:33,200
But the slag specks and the phosphorus stack up at the boundary between metal and rust, and a different layer begins to grow.

143
00:09:33,666 --> 00:09:36,913
First an amorphous layer called misawite.

144
00:09:36,913 --> 00:09:42,456
Then, slowly, a thin crystal layer of iron hydrogen phosphate hydrate.

145
00:09:42,456 --> 00:09:47,445
That crystal layer is dense and it hardly lets in water or air.

146
00:09:47,445 --> 00:09:49,266
Rust has nowhere to go.

147
00:09:49,733 --> 00:09:51,840
It needs help from the weather.

148
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:56,869
Alternate wetting and drying drives the formation of that phosphate layer.

149
00:09:56,869 --> 00:09:59,180
It cannot be switched on in a day.

150
00:09:59,180 --> 00:10:03,666
The crystal form grows slowly, which makes it a sign of great age.

151
00:10:04,133 --> 00:10:05,749
Verdict on clause four.

152
00:10:05,749 --> 00:10:07,506
Approved with conditions.

153
00:10:07,506 --> 00:10:12,004
The chemistry is real, but it is not lost, and it is not secret.

154
00:10:12,004 --> 00:10:15,729
It came from local ore, local wood, and no limestone.

155
00:10:15,729 --> 00:10:18,400
They did not need to know the formula.

156
00:10:18,866 --> 00:10:19,746
Clause five.

157
00:10:19,746 --> 00:10:21,139
The weather did it.

158
00:10:21,139 --> 00:10:25,540
An older theory says Delhi's climate is the deciding factor.

159
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:33,166
In nineteen seventy, the Swedish corrosion scientist Gosta Wranglen argued exactly that: dry, clean air.

160
00:10:33,633 --> 00:10:38,754
Rust runs faster when the air stays above about seventy percent humidity.

161
00:10:38,754 --> 00:10:41,841
Delhi, for most of the year, stays below it.

162
00:10:41,841 --> 00:10:47,033
Dry air really does slow iron down, and the pillar stands in the open air.

163
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:50,407
But the weather cannot be the whole story.

164
00:10:50,407 --> 00:10:54,561
Look at Konark, on the Odisha coast, with sea salt and damp.

165
00:10:54,561 --> 00:10:58,646
Its temple uses big iron beams from the thirteenth century.

166
00:10:58,646 --> 00:11:00,099
They are still there.

167
00:11:00,566 --> 00:11:09,478
Or Kollur, in the Kodachadri hills near India's humid west coast, where an old iron pillar stands at the Mookambika temple.

168
00:11:09,478 --> 00:11:15,346
Balasubramaniam counts it in the same family of corrosion-resistant ancient iron.

169
00:11:15,346 --> 00:11:17,013
Very different weather.

170
00:11:17,013 --> 00:11:18,100
Still standing.

171
00:11:18,566 --> 00:11:20,952
So, climate and metal work together.

172
00:11:20,952 --> 00:11:25,858
The dry air helps the pillar, and the phosphoric iron helps it everywhere.

173
00:11:25,858 --> 00:11:30,166
Neither explains the pillar alone, and neither needs a spaceship.

174
00:11:30,633 --> 00:11:33,999
There is one more catch for the miracle story.

175
00:11:33,999 --> 00:11:46,731
As Balasubramaniam noted in a study published in two thousand, no samples could be taken from the pillar, so lab work relied on rust samples, and an old Gupta-era iron clamp.

176
00:11:46,731 --> 00:11:49,000
The pillar itself is protected.

177
00:11:49,466 --> 00:11:51,121
Verdict on clause five.

178
00:11:51,121 --> 00:11:52,920
Approved with conditions.

179
00:11:52,920 --> 00:11:55,582
The climate is a helper, not a trick.

180
00:11:55,582 --> 00:12:02,633
It explains why Delhi is a lucky home, and why the damp coast iron beams are even more impressive.

181
00:12:03,100 --> 00:12:03,907
Clause six.

182
00:12:03,907 --> 00:12:05,670
Extraterrestrial origin.

183
00:12:05,670 --> 00:12:08,021
This one has a traceable author.

184
00:12:08,021 --> 00:12:20,066
Erich von Daniken's Chariots of the Gods, published in German in nineteen sixty-eight and in English in nineteen sixty-nine, used the pillar as proof of alien help.

185
00:12:20,533 --> 00:12:30,448
In nineteen seventy-four, an interviewer from Playboy told him that the column was not rust-free, and that its method of manufacture was understood.

186
00:12:30,448 --> 00:12:34,266
His answer: he no longer considered the pillar a mystery.

187
00:12:34,733 --> 00:12:36,963
One more mark deserves a mention.

188
00:12:36,963 --> 00:12:40,881
Partway up the shaft is a deep dent, made by a cannonball.

189
00:12:40,881 --> 00:12:49,733
There is no record from the time, but historians think it was probably fired in seventeen thirty-nine, on the orders of Nadir Shah.

190
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:52,919
The pillar took the hit and did not break.

191
00:12:52,919 --> 00:12:55,186
That is a stronger story than rust.

192
00:12:55,186 --> 00:12:59,266
Whatever this iron is, it took a cannon, and it stayed upright.

193
00:12:59,733 --> 00:13:01,360
Verdict on clause six.

194
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:01,878
Denied.

195
00:13:01,878 --> 00:13:06,537
Its main advocate gave it up himself, in nineteen seventy-four.

196
00:13:06,537 --> 00:13:11,566
The rust, the metal, and the method all have published explanations.

197
00:13:12,033 --> 00:13:12,665
Case file.

198
00:13:12,665 --> 00:13:15,133
Time to score the Iron Pillar of Delhi.

199
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:19,787
A Gupta-era inscription naming King Chandra: evidence.

200
00:13:19,787 --> 00:13:24,130
Forge-welded wrought iron, high in phosphorus: evidence.

201
00:13:24,130 --> 00:13:27,542
A thin, protective phosphate film: evidence.

202
00:13:27,542 --> 00:13:30,799
Delhi's dry climate as a helper: evidence.

203
00:13:31,266 --> 00:13:33,997
Never rusts: false, it rusts slowly.

204
00:13:33,997 --> 00:13:37,714
A lost super-metal: no, local ore and local wood.

205
00:13:37,714 --> 00:13:40,899
An alien alloy: dropped by its own author.

206
00:13:41,366 --> 00:13:42,273
The verdict.

207
00:13:42,273 --> 00:13:44,088
Human genius, not alien.

208
00:13:44,088 --> 00:13:51,271
A sixteen-hundred-year warranty honoured by phosphorus, slag, dry air, and a skilled workforce.

209
00:13:51,271 --> 00:13:52,027
Not magic.

210
00:13:52,027 --> 00:13:53,766
Not an accident either.

211
00:13:54,233 --> 00:13:57,543
And here is the part the aliens cannot match.

212
00:13:57,543 --> 00:14:00,411
This craft did not end with one pillar.

213
00:14:00,411 --> 00:14:09,899
According to Balasubramaniam and later studies, the iron at Dhar and the beams at Konark show the same phosphorus-rich signature.

214
00:14:10,366 --> 00:14:12,680
It is a monument to patient craft.

215
00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:23,366
People who never heard of phosphates built a pillar that outlasted empires, and it is still standing because they paid attention to what their materials did.

216
00:14:23,833 --> 00:14:27,526
Next case: a stone lid in a Maya tomb at Palenque.

217
00:14:27,526 --> 00:14:32,844
An astronaut at the controls of a rocket, or the world tree of the Maya?

218
00:14:32,844 --> 00:14:34,100
We open the tomb.

219
00:14:34,566 --> 00:14:37,425
Did you ever hear the rustless pillar story?

220
00:14:37,425 --> 00:14:43,466
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery, tested clause by clause.
